#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157019 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d20eac1d-b78f-5ac7-a3b7-fc72b15307a2" :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Non-Standard Relation"}] :question/id #uuid "682caa5d-ea1c-5701-b53d-c1e7c5419006" :question/math-academy-id "e-14593" :question/problem "Let $S = \\{3, 5 \\}$. Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $S$?\n\n$R = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in S^2 \\:: \\: y \\not \\mid 3x \\big\\}$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nA\\times A = A^2\n$$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in S^2$ such that $y \\not \\mid 3x$.\n\nLet's check each element $x \\in S$ to see which elements $y \\in S$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=3$, then the value $y=5$ satisfies $y \\not\\mid 3x$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,5)$.\n- If $x=5$, then there is no value of $y \\in B$ that satisfies $y \\not\\mid 3x$.\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(3,5) \\big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157020 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "481d9abf-1edd-5049-bae7-2e727fe3954c" :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Standard Relation"}] :question/id #uuid "40a594ed-35ce-52ed-817e-04398ad06b94" :question/math-academy-id "e-14596" :question/problem "Let $A=\\{1, 2\\}$ and consider the relation \"is greater than or equal to\" $(\\geq)$ on $A$. If this relation is given by the set $R$, express $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nA\\times A = A^2\n$$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A^2$ such that\n\n$$\nx\\geq y\n$$\n\nLet's check each element $x \\in A$ to see which elements $y \\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=1$, then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x\\geq y$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1)$.\n- If $x=2$, then the values $y=1$ and $y=2$ satisfy $x\\geq y$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,1)$ and $(2,2)$.\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR = \\{(1,1), (2, 1), (2, 2) \\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157021 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f77177ee-74b0-5681-92a8-ed6994ab9f9c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of Standard Relations"}] :question/id #uuid "aea9d065-3d1c-5b65-81ed-30682fc2b528" :question/math-academy-id "e-14629" :question/problem "Let $A=\\{-2, 0, 2\\}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $4$\" on $A$. Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nA\\times A = A^2\n$$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A^2$ such that $x \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$.\n\nLet's check each element $x \\in A$ to see which elements $y \\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=-2$, then the values $y=-2$ and $y=2$ satisfy $x \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$, since $4 \\mid (-2+2)$ and $4 \\mid (-2-2)$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(-2,-2)$ and $(-2,2)$.\n- If $x=0$, then the value $y=0$ satisfies $x \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$, since $4 \\mid (0-0)$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(0,0)$.\n- If $x=2$, then the values $y=-2$ and $y=2$ satisfy $x \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$, since $4 \\mid (2+2)$ and $4 \\mid (2-2)$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,-2)$ and $(2,2)$.\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(-2,-2), (-2,2), (0,0), (2,-2), (2,2)\\big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157022 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "0b2b99c2-a0fc-5194-9139-4348ef53d50a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14633" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$A = \\big\\{0, 1\\big\\}$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n$x\\:R\\: y \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad x\\neq y$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $0\\: R\\: 0$\n2. $0 \\: R\\: 1$\n3. $1 \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!}\\: 1$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nA\\times A = A^2\n$$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\in R$\n- $x \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!} \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if\n\n$$\nx\\neq y\n$$\n\nFor this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\neq$ are interchangeable, and\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ and $=$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\neq$, we have $0 \\neq 0$, which is false.\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\neq$, we have $0 \\neq 1$, which is true.\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ with the symbol $=$, we have $1 = 1$, which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
